#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125343} :activity/id #uuid "bfe361b1-984c-519f-8a6a-0ea85c15f6a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8cc35c33-61af-5ac3-971b-a33b8bbdcb9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1908} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125760 :tutorial/content "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "939069a1-d4fd-51b0-8c49-c1cdde533481" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3197 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "944f2e32-83df-59c4-9e62-dddd2a35c6d5" :step/math-academy-id 653140 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124298 :question/id #uuid "a788a6e6-8fb4-5657-912d-2eb8295a297f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4800"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415061}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"} :step/id #uuid "216d2c5a-6567-5c0a-8f69-8218d3f867f6" :step/math-academy-id 653148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124486 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124299 :question/id #uuid "7f0b6987-0ca3-5610-ab7b-6630c0f70191" :question/math-academy-id "e-4801"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdde35e9-7101-55f2-8452-ec1ef2d17d54" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407776}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Matrix Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "da0d1383-fa40-55ef-9635-eb9be7d2ca55" :step/math-academy-id 653144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125761 :tutorial/content "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n*A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x_1 \\ne 0,$ and we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n.\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." :tutorial/id #uuid "7600b92d-52bf-5c9a-bd48-5e9d12eb0d25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3198 :tutorial/title "An Important Property of Linearly Dependent Sets"} :step/id #uuid "307549b1-c36a-5e02-9555-3fe8462e3c33" :step/math-academy-id 653147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125344}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124487 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124300 :question/id #uuid "b8c2902e-9062-5417-9d19-a6a9dba986a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4803"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99ce8f12-ee79-5384-8eb9-e9bfbb2534c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407779}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Polynomial Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e2b92597-3696-53e3-94fd-361b7b47d46c" :step/math-academy-id 653141 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125345}}] :activity/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124708}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415062} :activity/id #uuid "a20e067c-d0eb-5b47-b6b0-79dbc97af27c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257 :topic/id #uuid "8cc35c33-61af-5ac3-971b-a33b8bbdcb9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1908} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1688faeb-85ba-5d68-81e7-78a4a337f227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415055} {:answer/id #uuid "7b430210-150a-5113-bb9e-9ceedc527951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415056} {:answer/id #uuid "888ec5b1-c2ea-5a99-8c7a-f1ea5fd2dbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415057} {:answer/id #uuid "a66c38ea-8581-5edc-9742-8946b8e3e270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415058} {:answer/id #uuid "f381c62f-1e63-5006-ba9d-a84a80c6800c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415056} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e50222-5322-5dc6-95c0-87aed25c41a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c07b7ca-2c8b-5a74-a857-c6a369e5fb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-35580" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{2}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{2}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}+{x}_{3}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{4}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "194a201e-a484-52b1-85fa-bf0a3ff539e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329740 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34fd27d7-929a-5d3e-9d4e-ea111063c513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327392} {:answer/id #uuid "517de5ba-6d5c-5c92-87e5-e6868a9f8439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327642} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b2b6f6-20f4-51e6-90c8-f7a66bb2f821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328523} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8a9fa8-a446-520d-84c9-1b72863959e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328860} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd080e3-b7ab-5e61-8794-2a6bd1f518bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329008} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a654311-5ad6-5322-8b43-d6568ca6f53a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d0d14302-1a26-551e-8f3a-e3f476256916" :question/math-academy-id "q-43827" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\text{ }{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent one, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{3}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{3}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{4}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "390603f7-3592-5dc2-9f55-cc90d76600ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8c14c0-682b-59df-8704-60dad9f468d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358136} {:answer/id #uuid "7e02e153-2ef7-5339-bb84-490ea18d5856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358848} {:answer/id #uuid "7f04c1e0-99fb-5138-9ed7-73aab13f1882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358863} {:answer/id #uuid "989b9ac8-f495-5fe9-8832-ec170320fa20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359090} {:answer/id #uuid "fc011385-3245-53db-bfd7-7dfedddbcb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358848} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0e9cb29-8c10-5947-bafa-898621240bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "611bd3b5-f7c4-573f-bd1b-b04a355b2452" :question/math-academy-id "q-94762" :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices ${A,B,C},$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O,$ where $O$ is the $2\\times 2$ zero-matrix." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} & -{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{3} & {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} & 6{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are  the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the the first equation to get \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-3{x}_{3}}{-2{x}_{3}}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "413d02a4-dfef-5bc6-b884-f744f0683a08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346852b7-6656-5a12-a46b-6592b5064052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407748} {:answer/id #uuid "56407750-29ee-542e-ab0f-c378d6fe430e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407750} {:answer/id #uuid "89572f45-44f7-54dc-824d-9dc887402248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407755} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f626e0-2b31-52fe-a111-63b2df1b6ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407761} {:answer/id #uuid "cf082154-5159-59fe-be15-43e70680a21e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407762}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407755} :answer-field/id #uuid "658cacd9-a35b-5416-9f49-41a93b0ebdc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "10b93a29-5953-5423-bb43-ce035ef4aec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-107026" :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices ${A,B,C},$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O,$ where $O$ is the $2\\times 2$ zero-matrix." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10{x}_{3} & -3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & -5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} & -{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}/2 \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{3}}=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bca21e33-420e-51ea-b2c1-ea2ef421580a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26908c88-4546-5c39-8a0d-31d5ea044633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241511} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea19c2f-a13c-5062-b135-dce37ac58281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242293} {:answer/id #uuid "893dfc03-9c7e-598a-9974-7948df60bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242388} {:answer/id #uuid "9a40e0a6-8ba3-5e19-a708-fa12bb1f7410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242527} {:answer/id #uuid "c64792d2-d8d6-53bc-9169-b3af2bc883ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242930}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242388} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e7f2f3-c8c1-5726-ad04-1013e80287b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bdbf8b9-5224-5509-b2d6-bdf2f04fc1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-107022" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3+9t.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3t)+{x}_{2}(-3+9t) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-3{x}_{2})+(-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2})t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot column is the $1$st one. Hence, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the $1$st equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{2}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ccfee361-efb3-56f5-ae8f-8f48353bd1c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415065}}] :activity/title "Linear Independence in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415054}]}
